# Tracer: exact dependent function carriers and compound anonymous values
#
# Before (Litex verified, but compiler emission stopped): a later parameter
# carrier could depend on an earlier source parameter. The old unary renderer
# had no exact target carrier for that dependency.
#
# Now the complete source layer is one dependent telescope. The second
# parameter carrier consumes the exact first argument and its R-membership
# witness; it is not flattened into an unrelated Lean function type.
forall f fn(x R, y {z R: z > x}) R:
    f = f

# The return carrier may depend on the source argument as well. Application
# unwraps the telescope only after checking a $in R, and f(a) has precisely
# the subtype carrier {z R: z > a}.
forall f fn(x R) {z R: z > x}, a R:
    f(a) = f(a)

# Compound anonymous bodies replay their own verifier-owned binder scope. The
# retained x $in R premise and x + 1 $in R closure build a native R-to-R value;
# equality does not replace the body with an identity-function shortcut.
fn(x R) R {x + 1} = fn(y R) R {y + 1}

# A direct anonymous application uses the exact FunctionHead WD child and its
# exact argument-membership proof, then reduces through the native Fn carrier.
forall a R:
    fn(x R) R {x + 1}(a) = fn(x R) R {x + 1}(a)

# Boundary: fn(x R) N {x} is rejected by Litex because x is not verified to
# belong to the declared N return carrier. The compiler never invents a cast
# or a proof to repair the missing source closure.
# Evidence: target/release/litex -compact -strict -runner -f lean/examples/24_DependentAnonymousFunction.lit
# and cargo test --release --test litex_to_lean_compiler_tracers
